  "prediction": "**Summary of the Department of Defense (DOD) Workforce Report**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) has undergone significant changes in its workforce composition and size since 2001. The DOD's military, civilian, and contractor workforces peaked around 2011 and have since decreased in size. The report highlights the challenges faced by the DOD in managing its workforce, including varying levels of U.S. involvement in conflicts, military to civilian conversions, and contractor insourcing.\n\nThe report assesses the DOD's efforts to compare the costs of its civilian and contractor workforces. The DOD developed a methodology to compare the costs of selected federal civilians and contractors, but the report finds that the DOD only partially addressed the mandated reporting elements. The DOD excluded certain non-labor costs, such as facility overhead, from its cost calculations, which is inconsistent with the guidance provided by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the DOD's own instruction.\n\nThe report also highlights the challenges faced by the DOD in compiling accurate and complete data on its workforce costs. The DOD's Cost-Comparison Report identified several challenges, including the lack of direct billing rates for contractors and the difficulty in mapping contractor personnel to specific functions.\n\nThe report makes several recommendations to the DOD, including:\n\n1. Develop a comprehensive and accurate methodology for comparing the costs of civilian and contractor workforces.\n2. Include all labor and non-labor costs in cost calculations.\n3. Improve the accuracy and completeness of data on workforce costs.\n4. Develop a plan to address the challenges identified in the Cost-Comparison Report.\n\nThe report concludes that the DOD has made progress in addressing some of the challenges faced by its workforce, but more work is needed to ensure that the DOD has accurate and complete data on its workforce costs and to develop a comprehensive and accurate methodology for comparing the costs of civilian and contractor workforces.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n* The DOD's workforce has decreased in size since 2011.\n* The DOD's methodology for comparing the costs of civilian and contractor workforces is incomplete and inaccurate.\n* The DOD excluded certain non-labor costs from its cost calculations.\n* The DOD faces challenges in compiling accurate and complete data on its workforce costs.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n1. Develop a comprehensive and accurate methodology for comparing the costs of civilian and contractor workforces.\n2. Include all labor and non-labor costs in cost calculations.\n3. Improve the accuracy and completeness of data on workforce costs.\n4",
